---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import SubNav from "../../../components/SubNav.astro";
import Section from "../../../components/Section.astro";
import Card from "../../../components/Card.astro";

const fragment = `{
  "fragment": "ossuary-b",
  "hexes":    [ { "q": 0, "r": 0, "surface": "floor" }, ... ],
  "edges":    [
    { "a": [0,0], "b": [1,0], "kind": "wall" },
    { "a": [0,0], "b": [0,1], "kind": "door", "port": "entry" }
  ],
  "occupants":[ { "at": [0,0], "block": "altar" } ],
  "points":   [ { "at": [1,0], "role": "spawn", "ref": "skeleton" } ]
}`;

const hexShape = `one hex (q, r, z)
  surface     floor | water | pit | void
  occupant    altar | chest | trap | vein | spawn     (at most one)
  edge[0..5]  wall | door | gate | window | curtain | none
  points      marker | patrol node | trigger | light   (any number)`;
---

<Base title="Dungeon building blocks — Party Dungeon" current="/mechanics/dungeon/">
  <SubNav current="/mechanics/dungeon/" />

  <header>
    <div class="kicker">
      <a href="/mechanics/">Mechanics</a> /
      <a href="/mechanics/dungeon/">Your dungeon</a> / Building blocks
    </div>
    <h1>A dungeon is a hex, six edges, and the things standing on it.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Nine behaviours on a hex grid. The grid changes the model more than the
      angle: walls stop being cells, and the map is already the graph.
    </p>
  </header>

  <Section title="There is no room type">
    <div class="grid">
      <Card status="decided" title="A room is not a building block" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          A room is a cluster of hexes, the edges around them, and a door.
          Making it a type means a block that secretly contains other blocks
          &mdash; and then two ways to say the same thing, which always drift
          apart.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="A room is a region the generator derives">
        <p>
          After placement, flood-fill the walkable hexes bounded by walls and
          closed thresholds. That region is what spawning, lighting, AI and loot
          budgets refer to. It is computed, never stored as a thing you can place.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="A reusable room is a saved arrangement">
        <p>
          The authored <code>boss_room: "ossuary-b"</code> on the data page is a
          fragment: hexes, edges and occupants with coordinates, kept in the
          blueprint document. It is a <em>layout</em>, not a type &mdash; reuse
          without inventing a composite.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Naming is not typing">
        <p>
          A generator can still require "one boss region, three treasure
          regions". The name hangs on a region query, so it costs nothing and
          cannot contradict the hexes on the grid.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Nine behaviours">
    <p class="lede" style="font-size:16px; margin-bottom:18px">
      Everything you place is one of these. The type is a behaviour, not a
      theme &mdash; which is why a wooden door and a stone door are the same
      type with different prefabs.
    </p>
    <div class="grid">
      <Card status="guess" title="Surface">
        <p>
          The hex's ground: floor, water, pit, void. Owns level changes &mdash;
          a ramp is a surface with a height delta, not a separate kind of thing.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Barrier">
        <p>
          Wall, fence, window. Lives on an <strong>edge</strong>, not in a hex.
          Two independent flags: <code>blocks_movement</code> and
          <code>blocks_sight</code>.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="decided" title="Threshold" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          Door, gate, secret door, portcullis, rubble, curtain, ladder, portal.
          Crosses an edge when a condition holds &mdash; the only type whose
          runtime state has to persist.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Fixture">
        <p>
          Chest, lever, pressure plate, altar, brazier, workbench, bed. Sits in
          a hex or on an edge and can be operated. A chest is a fixture with
          contents; a lever is a fixture that opens a threshold.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Hazard">
        <p>
          Spikes, flame vent, acid pool, collapsing floor, dart wall. Passable
          or not, but it costs something. Its trigger is data: proximity,
          weight, timer, or a linked lever.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="decided" title="Resource" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          Herb, ore vein, mushroom, fountain, tree. Harvestable. Respawn is
          per-instance, so "already picked" is instance state, not layout.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="decided" title="Spawn" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          A point that creates actors at runtime: mob, boss, NPC, captive,
          patrol. The species is a definition reference. The mobs themselves are
          never part of the layout.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="decided" title="Item" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          Loose drop, key, quest object. A portable reference sitting in a hex
          or inside a container. Keys are items that satisfy a threshold
          condition.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Marker">
        <p>
          Entry, exit, patrol node, trigger volume, arena bounds, light, sound.
          Invisible authoring data. Occupies a point and can share a hex with
          anything.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="One hex, six edges">
    <p class="lede" style="font-size:16px; margin-bottom:18px">
      On a square grid the wall was a cell and the boundary belonged to nobody.
      A hex has six sides, so the boundary becomes the natural place to put it.
    </p>
    <pre style="margin-bottom:16px"><code>{hexShape}</code></pre>
    <div class="grid">
      <Card status="decided" title="Hexes, axial coordinates" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          Cells are hexes, addressed as <code>(q, r)</code>, with
          <code>z</code> for level. Six neighbours, distance
          <code>(|dq| + |dr| + |dq+dr|) / 2</code>, and 60&deg; turns instead of
          90&deg;.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Walls are edges, not hexes">
        <p>
          A wall is a thin object on the boundary between two hexes. The other
          choice &mdash; solid rock hexes &mdash; doubles the map and makes a
          thin wall impossible. Here every hex is playable space or void.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Three occupancy classes, not four">
        <p>
          <b>Cell</b> &mdash; one occupant, exclusive. <b>Edge</b> &mdash; walls,
          doors and everything that hangs on them. <b>Point</b> &mdash;
          anywhere, any number. The square model's fourth class collapsed
          because walls moved to where torches were always going to hang.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="An edge belongs to a pair, not a side">
        <p>
          Identify an edge by the two hexes it separates, not by "north side of
          this hex". Two neighbours then cannot disagree about whether there is
          a wall between them, because there is only one row.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Cube maths is solved">
        <p>
          Store axial, compute in cube (<code>x + y + z = 0</code>). Rotation,
          rings, lines and distance are all standard; the <code>hexx</code> crate
          covers conversions, lines, rings and distance so none of this is
          bespoke.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Snapping is fragment ports, not prefab sides">
    <pre style="margin-bottom:16px"><code>{fragment}</code></pre>
    <div class="grid">
      <Card status="decided" title="Blocks declare how they join" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          Snapping is object metadata, not editor magic. On a hex grid it moves
          up a level: a wall or a door is placed on an edge, and an arrangement
          publishes the doors it is willing to be entered through.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Fragments carry the ports">
        <p>
          A saved arrangement declares which boundary edges are
          <code>entry</code>, <code>exit</code> or <code>through</code>. Two
          fragments connect when a port meets a port of the same kind and width.
          Nothing else has to match, so a doorway never needs to be in a set
          position.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Rotation is 60&deg;, and symmetry removes options">
        <p>
          Six orientations, but only the distinct ones. A through-corridor has
          two, not six; a round pillar has one. The editor offers distinct
          rotations so authors don't cycle through identical copies.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Footprints are sets of hexes">
        <p>
          A three-hex altar or an L-shaped ledge is a footprint expressed as
          offsets from an anchor hex, not a rectangle. Hex footprints have no
          convenient bounding box, so the set <em>is</em> the footprint.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Attachments are anchored ids">
        <p>
          A torch is "edge between (0,0) and (0,1), height 2"; a chest is
          "(1,1)". Edge and hex coordinates are stable ids, which is exactly
          what instance state needs to say "this gate is open".
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Entry is required, exit is optional">
        <p>
          A blueprint declares at least one <code>entry</code> port. With no
          distinct exit, the entry doubles as one &mdash; and a portal threshold
          is always a third way out. Entry and exit are port roles, not types.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Gates are edges with a condition">
    <div class="grid">
      <Card status="decided" title="Gates are not special" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          A gate is a threshold whose condition is unsatisfied at spawn. Nothing
          about the edge changes &mdash; only whether it opens, and what has to
          happen first.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Conditions are data; state is instance">
        <p>
          Lever, key, event (boss dead), timer, party size, puzzle. Which one is
          a field on the edge. Whether it is open is a row in the instance
          delta, keyed by the hex pair &mdash; so it survives a restart and dies
          with the instance.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Every gate must be solvable at generation">
        <p>
          Validate the entry-to-exit graph twice: once ignoring conditions (is
          the shape connected?) and once with them (can they actually be
          opened?). A gate whose key spawns behind it is a generation bug, not a
          player problem.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Sight and movement are different gates">
        <p>
          A portcullis stops your feet but not your eyes. A curtain does the
          reverse. Two flags on every edge, and they decide whether the mob on
          the other side has noticed you.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="The map is already the graph">
    <div class="grid">
      <Card status="guess" title="The dual is literal">
        <p>
          Hexes are nodes, edges are edges. On a square grid the layout had to be
          converted into a graph for pathfinding; here it already is one, so
          there is no second representation to drift out of sync.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Distance is hex distance">
        <p>
          Aggro radius, leash length and "is this reachable" all use cube
          distance, not a Euclidean approximation. A radius-4 alert is six rings
          of hexes, which is what the player sees.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Line of sight is an edge walk">
        <p>
          Draw the line between hex centres; every edge it crosses is checked
          once. A wall blocks sight, a portcullis does not. On a hex grid this
          is exact rather than a sampled ray, which is why hexes suit stealth.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Corridors zigzag, and that is the look">
        <p>
          A straight run of hexes alternates direction &mdash; there is no
          straight line of hex centres. Long halls need two rows, or a hall
          surface that deliberately breaks the pattern.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Population is not layout">
    <div class="grid">
      <Card status="guess" title="Spawn points outlive the mobs">
        <p>
          The spawn is a point on the grid; the mob is a runtime actor. Instance
          state records which spawns are cleared, so re-entering does not
          respawn the boss and does not need the boss in the record.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Items have two homes">
        <p>
          In a hex, as a cell placement. Or inside a container, where the
          contents come from a loot table plus a seed &mdash; and the instance
          only stores what was taken, not what could have been.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Harvest flags are the same as loot flags">
        <p>
          A picked herb patch, an opened chest and a dead boss are all "this
          thing is spent" in the instance delta. One mechanism, three names, no
          separate persistence path.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Database shape">
    <Card>
      <ul class="plain">
        <li><b>block_definitions</b> &mdash; kind, footprint hexes, traversal, placement rules, prefab reference.</li>
        <li><b>fragments</b> &mdash; a saved arrangement: hexes, edges, occupants, points, boundary ports.</li>
        <li><b>blueprints.layout</b> &mdash; the same four lists, plus fragment placements and rotations.</li>
        <li><b>blueprints.graph</b> &mdash; nodes and edges, derived at publish, stored for validation and load.</li>
        <li><b>instance.state.edges</b> &mdash; open, closed, locked, broken, keyed by hex pair.</li>
        <li><b>instance.state.spent</b> &mdash; chests opened, spawns cleared, nodes harvested.</li>
      </ul>
    </Card>
    <div class="grid" style="margin-top:16px">
      <Card status="guess" title="Edges are rows too">
        <p>
          One row per hex pair, not a bitmap. "Every door in this blueprint" and
          "every gate this lever opens" become queries instead of decodes, and
          an edge inherits the immutability of everything around it.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Fragments are first-class, rooms are not">
        <p>
          Fragments get ids, owners and versions because authors reuse them.
          Rooms stay derived because nobody can agree where one ends &mdash; and
          a stored room would have to be updated every time a hex next to it
          changed.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Retired, not deleted, again">
        <p>
          A block or fragment that falls out of use gets <code>retired_at</code>.
          Old blueprints still render, and old instances still validate, because
          nothing they point at has vanished.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Open questions">
    <Card>
      <ul class="plain">
        <li>
          <b>Is the hex a building unit, or a movement grid?</b> Authoring on
          hexes with continuous free movement is fine &mdash; collision still
          comes from hulls. Step-by-step movement is a different game and a much
          larger one.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>How big is a hex?</b> Two metres across makes tight corridors and
          honest mobs; four metres makes rooms need fewer pieces. Every block and
          every mob is authored against this number.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Rooms can't be rectangular any more.</b> A long hall is two rows of
          hexes or a special surface. Is that the look, or do some spaces need to
          opt out of the grid?
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>How thick is a wall?</b> Extruded edges are thin; too thin and a
          corridor of adjacent hexes reads as one wide space instead of two
          narrow ones.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Do fragments rotate freely?</b> A fragment rotated 60&deg; moves its
          entry port somewhere else, which may be fine for a generator and wrong
          for an authored setpiece.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Do players build with the same kit as the generator?</b> If yes, the
          editor needs the port rules and validation. If no, two kits drift and
          the authored ones rot.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </Card>
  </Section>
</Base>
